```
ROLE: product_realist  VOTE: yellow
REAL_USE_CASE: RAG system builders who report recall@k as headline retrieval metric while using fixed-size chunking with overlap < typical answer span length (15-40+ tokens). Occurs during eval harness setup for long-form QA (e.g., Natural Questions long answers, HotpotQA) where gold spans exceed 10-20% chunk overlap. Not relevant for teams already measuring downstream EM/F1 or using semantic chunking with adequate overlap.
OPERATIONAL_SIGNIFICANCE: Conditional real win, not microbenchmark-only. Mechanism (split → overcount) is exact and survives in production, but L0 magnitude is upper-bound inflated by always-retrievable-fragment assumption (lexical BM25 with unique tokens; real dense retriever misses fragments, shrinking gap). Score distortion >20pts confirmed at standard configs (chunk64/ov0/span16: 100% vs 78.5%; chunk32/ov0/span32: 100% vs 3.5%), but k-invariance and 1.000 recall are sim artifacts. Real RAG with top-k>1 and LLM reader can synthesize across multiple fragment chunks, making completeness@k (single-chunk whole-span) overly strict vs. practical answerability.
ADOPTION_BARRIER: [Requires gold span offsets to compute completeness@k (annotation cost); if you have offsets, downstream QA EM/F1 is superior signal and makes retrieval metric moot; overlap>=span_len eliminates overcount but increases index size/cost by ~20-50%; semantic/recursive chunking (LangChain, LlamaIndex) reduces splits but not standard in all pipelines; completeness@k not implemented in RAGAS/standard harnesses (context recall allows multi-chunk attribution); practitioner folklore already warns "increase overlap" without needing formal metric]
RATIONALE: Baseline IS real standard practice — recall@k/hit@k is the headline retrieval metric in RAG eval (e.g., RAGAS context recall, TowardsDataScience "How to Evaluate Retrieval Quality in RAG Pipelines", IBM community blog on retrieval metrics). Not a strawman like CLAIM-0042. Completeness@k is NOT standard practice nor obvious fix — RAGAS context recall measures attribution across chunks, not single-chunk containment; no citation found for completeness@k in prior art (searched arXiv, RAGAS docs, GitHub). Gap is real but conditional: overlap>=span_len → overcount=0 (control passes exactly), and short spans + adequate overlap → claim FALSE. Magnitude sim-inflated per honest caveat #1 (always-retrievable fragment). Actionable guidance: measure span length distribution vs. overlap; if overlap < p95 span length, either increase overlap or report completeness@k alongside recall@k; better, measure downstream reader EM/F1 which captures true cost of fragmentation.
```
